  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Cani Plouf occupies the Crique du Moulin, at the left end of Garonne beach in Le Pradet: a secluded spot, away from swimmers and boaters, presented by the Provence Méditerranée tourist office as a space for dogs and their owners.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The tourist office specifies that animals are accompanied by their owners, who see to hygiene rules. It adds that, apart from Cani Plouf, dogs on a leash are tolerated on Le Pradet's other beaches from 1 November to 31 March. Warning: the town hall, for its part, states on the Garonne beach page that access for any animal, even on a leash, is banned there (municipal order of 8 November 2023), and other sources say Le Pradet bans dogs from its beaches. The two pieces of information don't match: call the town hall (04 94 08 69 47) before swimming anywhere other than the dedicated cove.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: outside the Crique du Moulin, assume Le Pradet's beaches are closed to dogs, unless the town hall confirms otherwise.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Le Pradet's coastal path is nearby, but the town hall reports that a stretch between the Monaco and Bonnettes beaches is closed to pedestrians.</p>
    </div>

